    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 50.0, 241.639, 562.0, 262.639 ],
    "font" : "AdvP6975",
    "font size" : 8.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "FIG. 3. Partial density of states of YOX (X ¼ F, Cl, Br, and I) compounds calculated using TB-mBJ functional with WIEN2k code. The valence bands are mainly due to hybridized Op and Fp states whereas the conduction band is due to Yd states in all the compounds."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 200,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 48.333, 181.972, 298.667, 228.305 ],
    "font" : "AdvP6975",
    "font size" : 10.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]",
    "content" : "red shift of the phonon peak, particularly in the low energy region. The frequencies become smaller on moving from YOF to YOI which is due to increase in the mass of the halides."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 201,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 49.667, 155.305, 299.0, 180.639 ],
    "font" : null,
    "font size" : 12.0,
    "content" : "All the computed mode frequencies were real. Absence of modes with imaginary frequency clearly shows that all the compounds   studied here are dynamically   stable. and E modes correspond to motion of Y, X (X=F, CI) , and O atoms, respectively, in case of YOF, YOCI whereas in case of YOBr, YOI these modes correspond to displacement Y, and 0 atoms Similarly the vibra(1) (3) A belong to the antisymmetric vibration of Y, F and O atoms, respectively, in case of YOF, (1) 2) whereas in case of YOX (CI, Br, I) modes correspond to antisymmetric vibration of Y and X (CI, Br, and I). The mode corresponds to antisymmetric vibration of atoms E(1) . E(2) . B2g"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 202,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 50.0, 121.972, 298.333, 141.305 ],
